steps.body:
In `App.jsx`, derive two arrays from your tasks state:

```js
const activeTasks = tasks.filter(t => !t.completed)
const completedTasks = tasks.filter(t => t.completed)
```

Render them in two separate `<section>` elements with headings. Completed tasks should visually move out of the active list when checked.

Add a `useEffect` that runs every time tasks changes:

```js
useEffect(() => {
  localStorage.setItem('tasks', JSON.stringify(tasks))
}, [tasks])
```

Then change your `useState` initial value:

```js
useState(() => {
  const saved = localStorage.getItem('tasks')
  return saved ? JSON.parse(saved) : []
})
```

Refresh the page — your tasks should survive.

OpenWeatherMap returns deeply nested data with inconsistent field names and units. Your frontend should never know or care about OWM's raw shape — it should receive a clean, predictable object.

Write a `normalise(owmResponse)` function that extracts only what the UI needs:

```js
export function normalise(owm) {
  return {
    current: {
      temp:       Math.round(owm.current.temp),
      feelsLike:  Math.round(owm.current.feels_like),
      humidity:   owm.current.humidity,
      windSpeed:  Math.round(owm.current.wind_speed),
      condition:  owm.current.weather[0].description,
      icon:       owm.current.weather[0].icon,
    },
    hourly: owm.hourly.slice(0, 24).map(h => ({
      time:     new Date(h.dt * 1000).toISOString(),
      temp:     Math.round(h.temp),
      humidity: h.humidity,
    })),
    daily: owm.daily.slice(0, 5).map(d => ({
      date:      new Date(d.dt * 1000).toISOString(),
      high:      Math.round(d.temp.max),
      low:       Math.round(d.temp.min),
      condition: d.weather[0].description,
      icon:      d.weather[0].icon,
    })),
  }
}
```

Call `normalise(data)` in your route handler before returning. The key insight: OWM timestamps are Unix seconds, not milliseconds — multiply by 1000 before passing to `new Date()`.

If you ever switch weather providers, you only need to update this one function.

Install `node-cache`.

Create one cache instance with `stdTTL: 300` (5 minutes). Weather data doesn't change faster than that, and caching eliminates redundant upstream calls:

```js
import NodeCache from 'node-cache'
const cache = new NodeCache({ stdTTL: 300 })

app.get('/weather', async (request, reply) => {
  const { city } = request.query
  const cached = cache.get(city)
  if (cached) return cached

  // ... call OWM and normalise ...
  const result = normalise(data)

  cache.set(city, result)
  return result
})
```

The cache key is the city name. Use the lowercase version (`city.toLowerCase()`) so `London` and `london` hit the same entry.

This reduces upstream calls from potentially every keystroke to once per 5 minutes per city.

Two things can go wrong: OWM is down, or a user hammers your proxy. Handle both explicitly.

Register `@fastify/rate-limit` with a limit of 30 requests per minute per IP:

```js
await app.register(import('@fastify/rate-limit'), {
  max: 30,
  timeWindow: '1 minute'
})
```

Then wrap the OWM call in explicit status-code handling:

```js
const owmRes = await fetch(url)

if (owmRes.status === 404) {
  return reply.code(404).send({ message: 'City not found' })
}
if (!owmRes.ok) {
  request.log.error({ status: owmRes.status }, 'OWM upstream error')
  return reply.code(502).send({ message: 'Weather service unavailable' })
}

const data = await owmRes.json()
```

Never let raw OWM error messages (which include internal details) reach your client. Map them to generic messages before sending.

Also wrap the entire handler in try/catch and log unexpected errors with `request.log.error(err)` before re-throwing.

Create a `SearchBar` component with a controlled text input.

Without debounce, every keystroke fires a request. Add a 300ms delay using `useEffect` + `setTimeout`:

```js
useEffect(() => {
  if (!query.trim()) return
  const timeout = setTimeout(() => {
    fetchWeather(query)
  }, 300)

  return () => clearTimeout(timeout)
}, [query])
```

The cleanup function (`return () => clearTimeout`) cancels the pending timer whenever `query` changes before the 300ms expires. Only the final keystroke fires the request.

Store the search results in local state. Handle the loading state with a spinner inside the input — a visual cue that a request is in flight. This prevents a network request on every character typed.

Create a `ForecastRow` component that maps over the `daily` array (5 items) and renders a card per day.

```jsx
function ForecastRow({ daily }) {
  const fmt = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-GB', { weekday: 'short', day: 'numeric' })

  return (
    <div className="forecast-row">
      {daily.map((day, i) => (
        <div key={i} className="forecast-card">
          <div className="day">{fmt.format(new Date(day.date))}</div>
          <img
            src={`https://openweathermap.org/img/wn/${day.icon}@2x.png`}
            alt={day.condition}
          />
          <div className="temps">
            <span className="high">{day.high}°</span>
            <span className="low">{day.low}°</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  )
}
```

`Intl.DateTimeFormat` with `{ weekday: 'short', day: 'numeric' }` gives you "Mon 14" without any date library. It also respects the user's locale automatically — never write your own date formatter from scratch.

Style the high temp in a warm colour and the low in a cool colour to create immediate visual hierarchy.

Call geolocation inside a `useEffect` on mount:

```js
useEffect(() => {
  if (!navigator.geolocation) return

  navigator.geolocation.getCurrentPosition(
    (position) => {
      const { latitude, longitude } = position.coords
      fetchWeatherByCoords(latitude, longitude)
    },
    (error) => {
      console.warn('Geolocation denied:', error.message)
      setGeoError('Enable location for automatic weather, or search for a city above.')
    }
  )
}, [])
```

Add a `/weather?lat=&lon=` variant to your proxy that skips the geocoding step and calls OWM directly with coordinates.

If the user denies permission, show the `geoError` message below the search bar — don't silently fail. The app should still be fully functional via search when geolocation is unavailable.

A `useFavourites` hook centralises all the localStorage logic and keeps your components clean:

```js
function useFavourites() {
  const [favourites, setFavourites] = useState(() => {
    try {
      return JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem('favourites') ?? '[]')
    } catch {
      return []
    }
  })

  const add = (city) => {
    if (favourites.includes(city) || favourites.length >= 5) return
    const next = [...favourites, city]
    setFavourites(next)
    localStorage.setItem('favourites', JSON.stringify(next))
  }

  const remove = (city) => {
    const next = favourites.filter(c => c !== city)
    setFavourites(next)
    localStorage.setItem('favourites', JSON.stringify(next))
  }

  return { favourites, add, remove }
}
```

The lazy initialiser (`useState(() => ...)`) reads from localStorage only once on mount — not on every render.

Render saved cities as clickable chips above the search bar. Clicking one immediately loads that city's weather. When the list is at 5 items, disable the star button and show a tooltip: "Remove a city to add another".

Every data-fetching operation must render three states — not just the success case.

Use a single `status` string (`'idle' | 'loading' | 'error' | 'success'`) to drive all three:

```jsx
function WeatherPage() {
  const [status, setStatus]   = useState('idle')
  const [weather, setWeather] = useState(null)
  const [error, setError]     = useState(null)

  if (status === 'idle')    return <EmptyState />
  if (status === 'loading') return <WeatherSkeleton />
  if (status === 'error')   return <ErrorState message={error} onRetry={retry} />
  return <WeatherDisplay weather={weather} />
}
```

For `WeatherSkeleton`, use grey boxes in the exact shape of the real content — a rectangle where the temperature will be, a row where the forecast cards will be. This prevents layout shift when data loads.

For `ErrorState`, include a **Retry** button that re-fires the last request using a stored callback — don't make users re-type their search.

For `EmptyState`, show a prompt: "Search for a city or enable location to get started". Blank white screens are not acceptable states.

Install nanoid and write a `generateSlug` function that handles collisions and validates custom slugs.

```js
import { nanoid } from 'nanoid'

export async function generateSlug(db, customSlug) {
  if (customSlug) {
    if (!/^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{3,20}$/.test(customSlug)) {
      throw new Error('Invalid slug format')
    }
    return customSlug
  }

  for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
    const slug = nanoid(7)
    const { rows } = await db.query(
      'SELECT 1 FROM links WHERE slug = $1', [slug]
    )
    if (rows.length === 0) return slug
  }
  throw new Error('Failed to generate unique slug after 5 attempts')
}
```

Collisions are extremely rare with nanoid but you must handle them. Store slugs in lowercase. The loop retries up to 5 times before giving up.

The redirect handler must be fast. Record the click asynchronously — never `await` it inside the handler.

```js
export async function redirectSlug(request, reply) {
  const { slug } = request.params
  const db = request.server.db

  const { rows: [link] } = await db.query(
    'SELECT id, original_url FROM links WHERE slug = $1', [slug]
  )
  if (!link) return reply.code(404).send({ error: 'Not Found' })

  // Fire-and-forget — never await inside the redirect handler
  db.query(
    `INSERT INTO clicks (link_id, clicked_at, referrer, user_agent)
     VALUES ($1, NOW(), $2, $3)`,
    [link.id, request.headers.referer ?? null, request.headers['user-agent'] ?? null]
  ).catch(() => {})

  return reply.redirect(302, link.original_url)
}
```

The redirect must respond in under 100ms. Click tracking is a side effect that should never slow down the user.

Add JSON Schema validation to `POST /links` and register `@fastify/rate-limit`.

```js
const createLinkSchema = {
  body: {
    type: 'object',
    required: ['url'],
    properties: {
      url:        { type: 'string', format: 'uri' },
      customSlug: { type: 'string', pattern: '^[a-zA-Z0-9_-]{3,20}$' }
    },
    additionalProperties: false
  }
}
```

Register rate limiting on `POST /links`: max 10 requests per minute per IP. Return a clear error message when the limit is hit so the frontend can display it to the user.

Validate the URL client-side before sending — use the URL constructor, not a regex.

```js
function isValidUrl(input) {
  try { new URL(input); return true }
  catch { return false }
}

async function handleSubmit(e) {
  e.preventDefault()
  if (!isValidUrl(url)) return setError('Please enter a valid URL')
  setLoading(true)
  try {
    const { data } = await api.post('/links', { url, customSlug: slug || undefined })
    await navigator.clipboard.writeText(data.shortUrl)
    setResult(data)
  } catch (err) {
    setError(err.response?.data?.message ?? 'Failed to shorten URL')
  } finally {
    setLoading(false)
  }
}
```

Disable the submit button while `loading` is true to prevent double submissions.

Install `qrcode.react`. After a link is created, render the QR code and wire up the download button.

```jsx
import { QRCodeCanvas } from 'qrcode.react'

function LinkResult({ shortUrl }) {
  function downloadQR() {
    const canvas = document.querySelector('#qr-canvas canvas')
    const a = document.createElement('a')
    a.download = 'qr-code.png'
    a.href = canvas.toDataURL('image/png')
    a.click()
  }

  return (
    <div>
      <div id="qr-canvas">
        <QRCodeCanvas value={shortUrl} size={200} />
      </div>
      <button onClick={downloadQR}>Download PNG</button>
    </div>
  )
}
```

Use `QRCodeCanvas` (not `QRCodeSVG`) for downloads — canvas has a `.toDataURL()` method, SVG does not. Test the download on both Mac and Windows.

Using Vitest and supertest, test the entire user journey in one test.

```js
test('full redirect flow', async () => {
  // 1. Create a link
  const { body: link } = await request(app)
    .post('/links')
    .send({ url: 'https://example.com' })
    .expect(201)

  // 2. Redirect resolves to the original URL
  const res = await request(app)
    .get(`/${link.slug}`)
    .expect(302)
  expect(res.headers.location).toBe('https://example.com')

  // 3. Click was recorded
  const { body: analytics } = await request(app)
    .get(`/links/${link.id}/analytics`)
    .expect(200)
  expect(analytics.totalClicks).toBe(1)
})
```

If these three assertions pass, your app works. Ship nothing that breaks them.

Validate email and password length, then hash at 12 rounds regardless of whether the user exists.

```js
export async function register(request, reply) {
  const { email, password } = request.body

  const { rows: [existing] } = await db.query(
    'SELECT id FROM users WHERE email = $1', [email]
  )

  // Hash before the existence check — same response time either way
  const hash = await bcrypt.hash(password, 12)

  if (!existing) {
    await db.query(
      'INSERT INTO users (email, password_hash) VALUES ($1, $2)', [email, hash]
    )
  }

  // Always return the same response — never reveal whether an email is registered
  return reply.code(201).send({ message: 'Account created' })
}
```

`bcrypt.hash` at 12 rounds takes ~300ms — hashing before the existence check masks the timing difference that would otherwise let attackers enumerate valid emails.

Compare credentials in constant time. Issue the access token in the body and the refresh token as an httpOnly cookie.

```js
export async function login(request, reply) {
  const { email, password } = request.body
  const { rows: [user] } = await db.query(
    'SELECT id, password_hash, role FROM users WHERE email = $1', [email]
  )

  // Always compare — dummy hash prevents timing difference when user not found
  const DUMMY = '$2b$12$invalidhashpadding.000000000000000000000000000000000'
  const valid = await bcrypt.compare(password, user?.password_hash ?? DUMMY)
  if (!user || !valid) {
    return reply.code(401).send({ error: 'Invalid credentials' })
  }

  const accessToken = fastify.jwt.sign(
    { sub: user.id, role: user.role }, { expiresIn: '15m' }
  )
  const rawToken  = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex')
  const tokenHash = createHash('sha256').update(rawToken).digest('hex')
  const expiresAt = new Date(Date.now() + 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000)

  await db.query(
    'INSERT INTO refresh_tokens (user_id, token_hash, expires_at) VALUES ($1, $2, $3)',
    [user.id, tokenHash, expiresAt]
  )

  reply.setCookie('refreshToken', rawToken, {
    httpOnly: true, sameSite: 'Strict', secure: true,
    path: '/auth/refresh', maxAge: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60
  })
  return reply.send({ accessToken })
}
```

Access token goes in the response body (React stores it in memory). Refresh token goes in the cookie — `httpOnly` means JavaScript on the page can never read it.

Delete the old record and insert the new one atomically in a single transaction — this is rotation.

```js
export async function refresh(request, reply) {
  const raw = request.cookies.refreshToken
  if (!raw) return reply.code(401).send({ error: 'Unauthorized' })

  const hash   = createHash('sha256').update(raw).digest('hex')
  const client = await db.connect()
  try {
    await client.query('BEGIN')
    const { rows: [old] } = await client.query(
      `DELETE FROM refresh_tokens
       WHERE token_hash = $1 AND expires_at > NOW()
       RETURNING user_id`,
      [hash]
    )
    if (!old) {
      await client.query('ROLLBACK')
      reply.clearCookie('refreshToken', { path: '/auth/refresh' })
      return reply.code(401).send({ error: 'Invalid or expired token' })
    }
    const newRaw    = crypto.randomBytes(32).toString('hex')
    const newHash   = createHash('sha256').update(newRaw).digest('hex')
    const expiresAt = new Date(Date.now() + 7 * 24 * 60 * 60 * 1000)
    await client.query(
      'INSERT INTO refresh_tokens (user_id, token_hash, expires_at) VALUES ($1, $2, $3)',
      [old.user_id, newHash, expiresAt]
    )
    await client.query('COMMIT')
    const { rows: [user] } = await db.query(
      'SELECT role FROM users WHERE id = $1', [old.user_id]
    )
    const accessToken = fastify.jwt.sign(
      { sub: old.user_id, role: user.role }, { expiresIn: '15m' }
    )
    reply.setCookie('refreshToken', newRaw, {
      httpOnly: true, sameSite: 'Strict', secure: true,
      path: '/auth/refresh', maxAge: 7 * 24 * 60 * 60
    })
    return reply.send({ accessToken })
  } catch (err) {
    await client.query('ROLLBACK')
    throw err
  } finally {
    client.release()
  }
}
```

If two requests arrive with the same token simultaneously, only one wins the DELETE — the other gets 401. That is correct behaviour.

Logout deletes the token record and clears the cookie. The `requireRole` factory returns a preHandler.

```js
export async function logout(request, reply) {
  const raw = request.cookies.refreshToken
  if (raw) {
    const hash = createHash('sha256').update(raw).digest('hex')
    await db.query('DELETE FROM refresh_tokens WHERE token_hash = $1', [hash])
  }
  reply.clearCookie('refreshToken', { path: '/auth/refresh' })
  return reply.code(200).send({ message: 'Logged out' })
}

export function requireRole(allowedRoles) {
  return async function checkRole(request, reply) {
    await request.jwtVerify()
    if (!allowedRoles.includes(request.user.role)) {
      return reply.code(403).send({ error: 'Forbidden' })
    }
  }
}
```

Usage: `{ preHandler: [authenticate, requireRole(['admin', 'editor'])] }`. The factory pattern means allowed roles are defined once at route registration, not scattered through handler logic.

Register rate limiting on both auth routes and use constant-time comparison for any credential check.

```js
import { timingSafeEqual } from 'crypto'

// Use for any string comparison involving credentials or tokens
function safeCompare(a, b) {
  const bufA = Buffer.from(a)
  const bufB = Buffer.from(b)
  if (bufA.length !== bufB.length) return false
  return timingSafeEqual(bufA, bufB)
}

// Rate limit config — apply to /auth/login and /auth/register
await fastify.register(rateLimit, {
  max: 5,
  timeWindow: '1 minute',
  keyGenerator: (req) => req.ip,
  errorResponseBuilder: () => ({
    error: 'Too Many Requests',
    message: 'Too many login attempts — please wait a minute'
  })
})
```

Regular `===` comparison leaks information through response time. `timingSafeEqual` runs in constant time regardless of where the inputs diverge.

Store the access token in a module-level variable — never in React state, localStorage, or sessionStorage.

```js
let _accessToken = null  // module-level, outside React

export const AuthContext = createContext(null)

export function AuthProvider({ children }) {
  const [user, setUser]       = useState(null)
  const [isLoading, setLoading] = useState(true)

  function setToken(token) {
    _accessToken = token
    setUser(token ? jwtDecode(token) : null)
  }

  function getToken() { return _accessToken }

  // Silent refresh on mount — restores session after page reload
  useEffect(() => {
    api.post('/auth/refresh')
      .then(({ data }) => setToken(data.accessToken))
      .catch(() => setToken(null))
      .finally(() => setLoading(false))
  }, [])

  return (
    <AuthContext.Provider value={{ user, isLoading, setToken, getToken }}>
      {children}
    </AuthContext.Provider>
  )
}
```

LocalStorage and sessionStorage are readable by any JavaScript on the page. Module memory is only accessible to your own code, eliminating an entire class of XSS attack surface.

Intercept 401 responses and queue concurrent requests so only one refresh call is made.

```js
let isRefreshing = false
let queue = []

function processQueue(error, token = null) {
  queue.forEach(({ resolve, reject }) =>
    error ? reject(error) : resolve(token)
  )
  queue = []
}

api.interceptors.response.use(null, async (error) => {
  const original = error.config
  if (error.response?.status !== 401 || original._retry) {
    return Promise.reject(error)
  }

  if (isRefreshing) {
    return new Promise((resolve, reject) =>
      queue.push({ resolve, reject })
    ).then(token => {
      original.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${token}`
      return api(original)
    })
  }

  original._retry = true
  isRefreshing = true
  try {
    const { data } = await api.post('/auth/refresh')
    setToken(data.accessToken)
    processQueue(null, data.accessToken)
    original.headers.Authorization = `Bearer ${data.accessToken}`
    return api(original)
  } catch (err) {
    processQueue(err)
    clearToken()
    window.location.href = '/login'
    return Promise.reject(err)
  } finally {
    isRefreshing = false
  }
})
```

Without the queue, a page that fires 3 parallel requests will trigger 3 simultaneous refresh calls — each invalidating the previous token before it can be used.

Read from `AuthContext`. Show a spinner while loading. Gate on user existence first, then on role.

```jsx
function ProtectedRoute({ requiredRole, children }) {
  const { user, isLoading } = useAuth()
  const location = useLocation()

  if (isLoading) return <Spinner />

  if (!user) {
    return <Navigate to="/login" state={{ from: location }} replace />
  }

  if (requiredRole && user.role !== requiredRole) {
    return <ForbiddenPage />
  }

  return children
}
```

In `LoginPage`, redirect back to where the user came from after a successful login:

```js
const { from } = location.state ?? { from: { pathname: '/dashboard' } }
navigate(from, { replace: true })
```

Hide (do not just disable) features the user cannot access — disabled buttons still reveal what exists.

Before backend and frontend split off, the fullstack developer writes `protocol.md` defining every message type.

```
Message types:
  sync-step-1   client → server  { type: 'sync-1', sv: <state vector (base64)> }
  sync-step-2   server → client  { type: 'sync-2', update: <missing updates (base64)> }
  update        client → server  { type: 'update', update: <Yjs update (base64)> }
  awareness     client → server  { type: 'awareness', update: <awareness update (base64)> }

Close codes:
  4001  Authentication failed
  4004  Document not found
```

Define whether payloads are binary (Yjs default) or base64-encoded JSON. Both FE and BE must sign off on this document. Disagreements about the protocol after coding starts are very expensive to fix.

Register `@fastify/websocket`. On every upgrade, verify the JWT before the connection is established.

```js
fastify.get('/ws/:documentId', { websocket: true }, async (socket, request) => {
  // JWT from query string — cookies are not reliably sent on WS upgrades
  const token = request.query.token

  let user
  try {
    user = fastify.jwt.verify(token)
  } catch {
    socket.close(4001, 'Authentication failed')
    return
  }

  const { documentId } = request.params
  const doc = await loadOrCreateDoc(documentId)
  if (!doc) {
    socket.close(4004, 'Document not found')
    return
  }

  handleConnection(socket, user, documentId)
})
```

Attach the verified `user` object to the connection — never trust identity data sent by the client.

Maintain a Map of rooms in memory. On every incoming update, broadcast to all other connections in the same room.

```js
const rooms = new Map()  // Map<documentId, Set<WebSocket>>

function handleConnection(socket, user, documentId) {
  if (!rooms.has(documentId)) rooms.set(documentId, new Set())
  const room = rooms.get(documentId)
  room.add(socket)

  socket.on('message', (data) => {
    // Broadcast to all peers except sender
    for (const peer of room) {
      if (peer !== socket && peer.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) {
        peer.send(data)
      }
    }
  })

  socket.on('close', () => {
    room.delete(socket)
    if (room.size === 0) rooms.delete(documentId)
  })
}
```

This is the entire broadcast loop. Keep it simple before adding Redis — understand the single-instance case first.

Maintain one `Y.Doc` per document in memory. Apply incoming updates and broadcast to peers.

```js
import * as Y from 'yjs'

const docs = new Map()  // Map<documentId, Y.Doc>

async function loadOrCreateDoc(documentId) {
  if (docs.has(documentId)) return docs.get(documentId)

  const doc = new Y.Doc()

  // Load persisted state from DB on first connection
  const { rows: [row] } = await db.query(
    'SELECT yjs_state FROM documents WHERE id = $1', [documentId]
  )
  if (row?.yjs_state) {
    Y.applyUpdate(doc, row.yjs_state)
  }

  docs.set(documentId, doc)
  return doc
}

function handleUpdate(documentId, update) {
  const doc = docs.get(documentId)
  if (doc) Y.applyUpdate(doc, update)
}
```

The server doc is the authoritative merge point. Loading from the database on first connection ensures returning users see their previous state, not a blank document.

Start a persist interval when a room opens. Save every 30 seconds; take a snapshot every 5 minutes.

```js
const persistTimers    = new Map()  // Map<documentId, NodeJS.Timer>
const snapshotCounters = new Map()  // Map<documentId, number>

function startPersisting(documentId) {
  if (persistTimers.has(documentId)) return

  const timer = setInterval(async () => {
    const doc = docs.get(documentId)
    if (!doc) return
    const state = Y.encodeStateAsUpdate(doc)

    await db.query(
      'UPDATE documents SET yjs_state = $1, updated_at = NOW() WHERE id = $2',
      [state, documentId]
    )

    // Snapshot every 10 ticks (≈ 5 minutes)
    const count = (snapshotCounters.get(documentId) ?? 0) + 1
    snapshotCounters.set(documentId, count)
    if (count % 10 === 0) {
      await db.query(
        'INSERT INTO snapshots (document_id, yjs_state) VALUES ($1, $2)',
        [documentId, state]
      )
    }
  }, 30_000)

  persistTimers.set(documentId, timer)
}

function stopPersisting(documentId) {
  clearInterval(persistTimers.get(documentId))
  persistTimers.delete(documentId)
  snapshotCounters.delete(documentId)
}
```

Call `stopPersisting` when the room empties. Do one final persist before clearing the in-memory doc.

Install `ioredis`. Publish updates so other server instances can broadcast to their local connections.

```js
import Redis from 'ioredis'

const pub = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL)
const sub = new Redis(process.env.REDIS_URL)

sub.psubscribe('doc:*')

sub.on('pmessage', (_pattern, channel, message) => {
  const documentId = channel.replace('doc:', '')
  const room = rooms.get(documentId)
  if (!room) return
  for (const peer of room) {
    if (peer.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN) peer.send(message)
  }
})

// Call this when a local connection sends an update
function publishUpdate(documentId, update) {
  pub.publish(`doc:${documentId}`, update)
}
```

Two users connected to different server instances now collaborate — their updates route through Redis to each other's instance.

Install `y-websocket` and connect the `Y.Doc` to the server.

```bash
npm install y-websocket
```

```js
import { WebsocketProvider } from 'y-websocket'

const provider = new WebsocketProvider(
  `${import.meta.env.VITE_WS_URL}/ws`,
  documentId,
  ydoc,
  { params: { token: getToken() } }
)

// Show connection status in the UI
provider.on('status', ({ status }) => {
  // status: 'connecting' | 'connected' | 'disconnected'
  setConnectionStatus(status)
})
```

The provider handles the sync-step-1/2 handshake, delivers updates to the `Y.Doc`, and reconnects automatically with exponential backoff. You do not need to manage reconnection logic yourself.

Set the local user's awareness state and listen for peers' cursor changes.

```js
// Set local presence
provider.awareness.setLocalStateField('user', {
  name:  currentUser.display_name,
  color: userColor(currentUser.id)  // deterministic from user ID
})

// Listen to all peer state changes
provider.awareness.on('change', () => {
  const states = Array.from(provider.awareness.getStates().entries())
  const peers  = states
    .filter(([clientId]) => clientId !== ydoc.clientID)
    .map(([, state]) => state.user)
  renderCursors(peers)
})

// Deterministic colour — stable across sessions
function userColor(userId) {
  const hue = (userId * 137.508) % 360  // golden angle distribution
  return `hsl(${hue}, 70%, 50%)`
}
```

Using a deterministic colour means the same user always appears in the same colour — avoiding confusion when someone reconnects.

Fetch the snapshot list and display it in a sidebar. Restore by applying the snapshot state to a new `Y.Doc`.

```js
// Fetch snapshots list
const { data: snapshots } = await api.get(`/documents/${docId}/snapshots`)

// Preview a snapshot in a read-only editor
async function previewSnapshot(snapshotId) {
  const { data } = await api.get(
    `/documents/${docId}/snapshots/${snapshotId}`
  )
  const previewDoc = new Y.Doc()
  Y.applyUpdate(previewDoc, new Uint8Array(data.yjs_state))
  const text = previewDoc.getText('codemirror').toString()
  setPreviewContent(text)
}

// Restore a snapshot
async function restoreSnapshot(snapshotId) {
  await api.post(`/documents/${docId}/restore`, { snapshotId })
  // Server applies the snapshot to the live doc and broadcasts the update
  // The WebsocketProvider receives it and syncs automatically
}
```

The restore is handled server-side — apply the snapshot to the live `Y.Doc`, broadcast the full state update to all connected clients.
